<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Homework 04: Data Governance and Dimensionality</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Complete the exercises below and reflect on how data collection and analysis shape governance decisions.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <title>Principal Component Analysis</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A city agency collects data on five neighborhoods using four features: average income, public transit use, broadband access, and health clinic visits. The data for four neighborhoods is shown below.
        </p>
        <table>
          <title>Neighborhood Data</title>
          <tabular>
            <row><cell>Neighborhood</cell><cell>Income</cell><cell>Transit</cell><cell>Broadband</cell><cell>Clinic Visits</cell></row>
            <row><cell>A</cell><cell>45</cell><cell>30</cell><cell>80</cell><cell>20</cell></row>
            <row><cell>B</cell><cell>55</cell><cell>20</cell><cell>70</cell><cell>35</cell></row>
            <row><cell>C</cell><cell>30</cell><cell>50</cell><cell>60</cell><cell>40</cell></row>
            <row><cell>D</cell><cell>40</cell><cell>40</cell><cell>75</cell><cell>25</cell></row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <ol>
          <li><p>Center each feature by subtracting its mean. Write the centered data matrix.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute the covariance matrix for the centered data. Explain what each entry means in the context of neighborhood governance.</p></li>
          <li><p>Find the principal components of the dataset. Which direction captures the most variation?</p></li>
          <li><p>Describe how a planner might use PCA to decide which neighborhoods need attention and which features are most important.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <title>Data Governance Reflection</title>
      <statement>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Explain why the choice of features matters when a government agency uses data to allocate resources.</p></li>
          <li><p>What are the risks when data is collected unevenly across communities? Give one concrete example.</p></li>
          <li><p>Sports analytics can be repurposed to practice dimensionality reduction, but not all datasets are equally appropriate for public policy. Describe one way that a demographic dataset and one way that a sports dataset differ in governance relevance.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>
